Q: Why can my VS Code extension client not find a local module? A: <p>A project reference tells TypeScript how to type-check and build the referenced project. It does not rewrite the module specifier used at runtime.</p>
<p>After compilation, <code>client/src/extension.ts</code> becomes a file under <code>client/out</code>, but its import is still emitted as:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>require('../../shared/src/test');
</code></pre>
<p>Node therefore looks for JavaScript under <code>shared/src</code>, while the shared project actually emits <code>shared/out/test.js</code>. Node does not load the TypeScript source file from <code>shared/src</code> here.</p>
<p>One way to make compile-time and runtime resolution agree is to make <code>shared</code> a local package. For example, the root package can declare workspaces:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;private&quot;: true,
  &quot;workspaces&quot;: [&quot;client&quot;, &quot;server&quot;, &quot;shared&quot;]
}
</code></pre>
<p>Give the shared package runtime and type entry points:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;bg3-osiris-shared&quot;,
  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;1.0.0&quot;,
  &quot;private&quot;: true,
  &quot;main&quot;: &quot;out/test.js&quot;,
  &quot;types&quot;: &quot;out/test.d.ts&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Declare it in <code>client/package.json</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {
    &quot;bg3-osiris-shared&quot;: &quot;file:../shared&quot;
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then import it by package name:</p>
<pre class="lang-ts prettyprint-override"><code>import { someString } from 'bg3-osiris-shared';
</code></pre>
<p>Run the root <code>npm install</code> so the workspace dependency is linked, then build with <code>tsc -b</code>. The emitted client code now contains <code>require('bg3-osiris-shared')</code>, and Node resolves the package's <code>main</code> entry to <code>shared/out/test.js</code>.</p>
<p>In a minimal reproduction, the original relative import builds successfully but fails at runtime with <code>MODULE_NOT_FOUND</code>; the package import builds and prints <code>hello world</code>.</p>
<p>For a VS Code extension that is distributed as one artifact, bundling the client and shared code with esbuild or webpack is another option. Keep the <code>vscode</code> module external, as shown in the <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/api/working-with-extensions/bundling-extension" rel="nofollow noreferrer">VS Code bundling guide</a>.</p>
<p>Reference: <a href="https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/project-references.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">TypeScript project references</a>.</p>
